{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3186","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3186","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3186\",\"parent_revision\":\"b618f58a3cb3c972b17e32265b1e23b34f9b57acc0e2fc1aadd9902506f6c71b\",\"root_revision\":\"b618f58a3cb3c972b17e32265b1e23b34f9b57acc0e2fc1aadd9902506f6c71b\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/tests_backlog/` contains 26 test files and 534 collectible tests. The directory is absent from `pyproject.toml`'s `[tool.pytest.ini_options] testpaths`, so none of them run in the default suite or in CI.\\n\\nMeasured, not inferred:\\n\\n```\\nuv run pytest --collect-only            \u2192 5321 tests collected, none from tests_backlog\\nuv run pytest .../tests_backlog --collect-only \u2192 534 tests collected\\n```\\n\\nThe standalone plugin runner does not pick them up either \u2014 `plugins/development-harness/tests/run_pytest.py` defaults to `[\\\"tests\\\", \\\"tests_sam\\\", \\\"sam_schema/tests\\\", \\\"backlog_core/tests\\\"]`.\\n\\nThe codebase is aware of the omission. `plugins/development-harness/conftest.py:200` refers to `tests_backlog` as a directory to handle \\\"once it joins\\\". `pyproject.toml` already lists `tests_backlog/*` in a coverage exclusion block, and `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_network_guard.py:211` parametrizes over `[\\\"tests\\\", \\\"tests_sam\\\", \\\"tests_backlog\\\", \\\"sam_schema/tests\\\"]` \u2014 so parts of the repo treat it as a live test root and the collector does not.\\n\\n## Current state of those tests\\n\\nRan directly:\\n\\n```\\nuv run pytest plugins/development-harness/tests_backlog -q\\n533 passed, 1 failed in 24.66s\\n```\\n\\nThe single failure is `test_github_sync_provider.py::test_github_content_provider_replay_preserves_concurrent_remote_revision`:\\n\\n```\\nassert current.content == \\\"revision-two\\\"\\nE   AssertionError: assert 'queued' == 'revision-two'\\n```\\n\\nA concurrent-revision defect in the GitHub content provider's replay path, caught by a test that has never run in CI.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n533 passing tests provide no protection, because nothing runs them \u2014 a regression against any of them lands silently.\\n\\nThe coverage they provide is not duplicated elsewhere. One concrete instance found while auditing artifact read/write coverage: `tests_backlog/test_local_content_backends.py::test_artifact_registration_publishes_matching_metadata_and_body` is the only test in the repo that chains `artifact_register` into `artifact_read` in one test with a real content assertion, against a real provider fixture rather than independently-mocked halves. Every other artifact test mocks the two halves separately, so none of them can fail if a real register-then-read round trip loses or corrupts content. That coverage exists and does not run.\\n\\nThis also produces wrong conclusions in coverage audits. An audit scoped to the directories the running suite collects reports \\\"no test covers X\\\" when a test covering X exists in this directory \u2014 leading someone to write a duplicate.\\n\\n## Not established\\n\\nWhy the directory was never added to `testpaths` \u2014 whether deliberate at the time and never revisited, or an omission when the directory was created. Whether the one failing test represents a live product defect or a stale test expectation; it has not been diagnosed, only observed.\\n\\n## User-provided context\\n\\nAdding the directory to `testpaths` is the obvious direction, but the failing test has to be resolved first or CI goes red on the change. Which of those two is the real defect \u2014 the provider or the expectation \u2014 is diagnosis work, not something to assume from the assertion text.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n533 passing tests provide no protection, because nothing runs them \u2014 a regression against any of them lands silently.\\n\\nThe coverage they provide is not duplicated elsewhere. One concrete instance found while auditing artifact read/write coverage: `tests_backlog/test_local_content_backends.py::test_artifact_registration_publishes_matching_metadata_and_body` is the only test in the repo that chains `artifact_register` into `artifact_read` in one test with a real content assertion, against a real provider fixture rather than independently-mocked halves. Every other artifact test mocks the two halves separately, so none of them can fail if a real register-then-read round trip loses or corrupts content. That coverage exists and does not run.\\n\\nThis also produces wrong conclusions in coverage audits. An audit scoped to the directories the running suite collects reports \\\"no test covers X\\\" when a test covering X exists in this directory \u2014 leading someone to write a duplicate.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation during E2E coverage review, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Current State Of Those Tests\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRan directly:\\n\\n```\\nuv run pytest plugins/development-harness/tests_backlog -q\\n533 passed, 1 failed in 24.66s\\n```\\n\\nThe single failure is `test_github_sync_provider.py::test_github_content_provider_replay_preserves_concurrent_remote_revision`:\\n\\n```\\nassert current.content == \\\"revision-two\\\"\\nE   AssertionError: assert 'queued' == 'revision-two'\\n```\\n\\nA concurrent-revision defect in the GitHub content provider's replay path, caught by a test that has never run in CI.\\n</div>\\n\\n## User Provided Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAdding the directory to `testpaths` is the obvious direction, but the failing test has to be resolved first or CI goe